course: dictionary
lesson: 11513
title: Akin: similar to something else
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When two things are much the same, you can say they are akin. Akin is nearly always used in the phrase «akin to». «The buzz in the crowd was akin to the sound of a far-off waterfall.» The crowd is not a waterfall, but its noise is similar to one.
explain: Use the words around akin to check its meaning. «The feeling in his stomach was akin to fear.» The words feeling and stomach tell you the feeling is close to fear, but not quite the same. Try swapping in «like»: «a feeling like fear» still makes sense, so akin means similar to.
explain: Akin has an old meaning too: related by family. «The two families were akin, because they shared a grandfather.» Here akin means the families were related by blood. You will mostly meet it in writing, so look for the word «to» after it.
fact: Akin is an adjective, and it is nearly always used in the phrase «akin to».
fact: «Akin to» means similar to, or much the same as.
fact: In «Her love for her cousin was akin to the love of a sister», akin shows the two feelings are close but not exactly the same.
fact: In an old use, «akin» means related by family, as in «We are akin through our grandmother».
fact: A good check: swap in «like» or «similar to». If the sentence still makes sense, akin means similar.
exercise: «The noise from the storm was akin to thunder.» What does akin mean here? | Hidden under | *Similar to | Cheerful about | Far away from
exercise: Which word is the best swap for akin in «a feeling akin to fear»? | away from | *like | happy about | under
exercise: «The two families were akin.» What does akin mean in this sentence? | Angry with each other | Living in the same town | *Related by family | Tired from travelling
test: «His excitement was akin to joy on Christmas morning.» What does akin mean? | Opposite to | Too loud for | Bored of | *Similar to
test: Which is the old meaning of akin? | Working hard | Light and glowing | *Related by family | Very hungry
exam: Which sentence uses akin correctly? | He akin to the table was very tall. | *Her grief was akin to a heavy stone in her chest. | Akin is a type of fruit that grows on trees. | The cat akin the dog ran away quickly.
